the terms high and low come up all the  time in Japanese pitch accent studies  but of course in reality there are much  more than just two tones speakers might  change their tone significantly based on  their emotions or intentions what  they're what they're trying to  communicate and I get a lot of questions  about this people say well I understand  that the pitch accent of the word is  supposed to be this but I'm hearing  something that's a little bit different  what's going on here now I break this  down in detail in lesson 6.3 of my  Japanese phonetic series on patreon it's  about a 20 minute lesson but there's a  seven or so minute chunk of that lesson  that I think would be very useful for a  lot of people to see so I like to share  it on YouTube today I have a couple of  comments that I'll share at the end of  the video but for now let's just get  right into it hi everyone and welcome to  episode 6.3 of Japanese phonetics by  dogen in this lesson we'll be talking  about binary graphics and spectrographs  in this series we use binary Graphics  when showing the pitch accent pattern of  a word that is to say the standard  Graphics that we use throughout this  series only show two tones high and low  this is normal practice essentially all  Japanese phonetic resources use binary  Graphics to indicate pitch accent some  do it with Graphics like this others use  Graphics like this and others still use  Graphics like this all these Graphics  are meant to display essentially only  one thing whether or not a word has an  accent and if it does where in the word  that accent occurs thus this binary  graphic for the word Japan for instance  shows me that the word has an accent on  its second Mora it does not show me how  a specific speaker has already said the  word and mapped their voice emotions and  intentions onto the word it might help  to think about things like this if I  look up the word America on  dictionary.com it gives me the  definition of the word as well as this  pronunciation key you see how the mare  section of this pronunciation key is in  bold this is meant to indicate that the  accent falls on this syllable of the  word now of course there are many  different correct ways to say America we  could say it in a happy way as in  America or we could say it in an angry  way as in America or we could say it as  a question as in America so depending on  what we're trying to say or emphasize  the pronunciation of the word as well as  the words pitch will change a little bit  however what doesn't change generally  speaking and this is particularly true  for one word phrases is the accent  location again whether it's America or  America or America the accent stays on  the Met it's sort of like the word has a  basic shape and then when we say the  word and map our emotions and intentions  onto it we sort of stretch and play with  that shape a little bit now  dictionary.com and other dictionaries  obviously can't show a spectrograph or a  computer-generated pitch wave for every  acceptable pronunciation of the word  America so instead it just shows this  again a key that shows where the accent  is the dictionary just shows the words  basic shape and then language Learners  learn this shape knowing that when a  speaker actually says the word they'll  be stretching and playing with it a  little bit binary Japanese pitch accent  Graphics are similar to this this  graphic shows me where the accent and  the word Japan is and if we had a native  speaker slowly and carefully say this  word as in  then created a spectrograph of this  recording it might look pretty similar  to this in fact let's take a look at the  spectrograph of that recording now  so pretty close right but while a  speaker could say me home  they could also say  nihong  or  or Nihon  etc etc but again it's impossible for a  pitch accent resource to list a  spectrograph for every acceptable  pronunciation of the word Japan so  instead pitch accent resources use  binary Graphics to indicate a word's  basic properties that is to say pitch  accent resources use binary Graphics to  indicate whether or not a word has an  accent and if it does where in the word  that accent occurs thus it's very  important that we Japanese Learners  realize that native speakers stretch and  play with these shapes and that because  of that what we hear won't always line  up perfectly with the kind of Graphics  that are used in this series as well as  in other pitch accent resources so this  idea that what you hear won't always  line up perfectly with the graphics that  are used in in my series and in other  pitch accent resources  oftentimes people again end up asking me  about this they say what's going on here  and hopefully now you have a better  understanding of exactly what's  happening of course there is some  individual and Regional dialects dialect  variation that can affect the the tone  and actual pitch action pattern of a  word but in my experience most of the  time when what you hear isn't lining up  with the pitch oxen pattern of the word  it's because the speaker has mapped  their emotions or intentions onto the  word they're giving the word intonation  and this in in this sense they're kind  of stretching and playing with the words  pitch accent pattern all right now back  to the video now I imagine that many of  you are now wondering two things one  exactly how do I map my emotions and  intentions onto a word and two if the  actual pitch of a word depends on how  the speaker is mapping their emotions  and intentions onto it then what's even  the point of studying pitch accent I'd  like to answer both of these questions  now first how do I map my emotions and  intentions onto a word good news you're  probably already doing this in a natural  way especially if you've been studying  Japanese for a while in my experience  Most Japanese Learners particularly  those that do a lot of immersion so  listening to Native recordings are good  at mapping their emotions and intentions  onto words they usually know how to say  words in a happy or sad or angry or  questioning way even though they've  never explicitly learned or studied how  to do this now I do give some basic tips  on this later on in the series but again  most people are naturally good at this  so if you've been studying Japanese for  a while again and you do a lot of  immersion then you're probably already  mapping your emotions and intentions  onto words in a more or less natural way  however and this is a big caveat even if  you naturally know how to stretch and  play with the shape of a word depending  on what you're trying to say if you're  stretching and playing with the wrong  shape to begin with  it's going to sound wrong that is to say  nihongo  and  nihongo  and  nihongo  are all right however  and  nihongo  and  nihongo  are all wrong in other words it's  relatively easy to map your emotions and  intentions onto a word and sound correct  if you already know that word's basic  properties and this is why we study  pitch accent we study pitch accents such  that when we're actually speaking and  mapping our emotions and intentions onto  words we're doing so with the correct  pitch accent patterns or shapes we study  using binary pitch acts and Graphics  like this  such that we can unconsciously say this  this  and this  Okay so we've learned why it's important  to study a pitch accent and also why  pitch accent resources use binary  Graphics rather than spectrographs to  teach pitch accent but is there any  useful information that we can learn  from spectrographs yes there is and for  the rest of today's lesson we'll be  talking about one of these topics so  that's a look at pitch oxen intonation  and why what you hear won't always line  up perfectly with what's on screen and  again this was a seven minute or so  sample clip from the full 20-minute  lesson which also covers a phenomenon  that I call the subtle drop in Pitch I  kind of 